      <pb n="75"/>
      <!-- the reindeer-colour list continues from 1-100, which ends at item 14;
           set in two columns, 15-21 left and 22-26 right, so the OCR line order
           interleaves them against the single printed 15-26 sequence.
           Typography, not structure. -->
      <list type="gloss" xml:id="list-1-101-1" prev="#list-1-100-1">
        <item n="15"><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-1">Elveˊek</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-1">“one hind-leg in white stocking” [body gray or brown]</gloss>).</item>
        <item n="16"><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" facs="#52" xml:id="term-1-101-2">Keˊmg̣ekem</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-2">“both hind-legs in white stockings”</gloss>).</item>
        <item n="17"><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-3">ᵉIᵋñɪt-qeˊñu</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-3">“nose-tip white” [literally, “nose-tip white mixed with brown”]</gloss>).</item>
        <item n="18"><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-4">Arê-qaˊño</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-4">“groin white mixed with brown”</gloss>).</item>
        <item n="19"><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-5">Rɪˊččit-qeˊñu</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-5">“belt white mixed with brown”</gloss>).</item>
        <item n="20"><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-6">Qeˊñu</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-6">“white mixed with brown”</gloss>).</item>
        <item n="21"><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-7">Meiˊñɪ-qeˊñu</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-7">“very much <foreign xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm">qeˊñu</foreign>,” i. e., more white than brown</gloss>).</item>
        <item n="22"><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-8">Keliˊlɪn</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-8">“spotted” [white spots on brown or gray ground]</gloss>).</item>
        <item n="23"><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-9">Rew-ᵉiˊᵋtɪn</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-9">“ptarmigan-neck” [white body with a black head, like a ptarmigan in spring plumage]</gloss>).</item>
        <item n="24"><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" facs="#18" xml:id="term-1-101-10">ɪᵋplɪˊlɪ-qeˊñu</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-10">“yellowish gray mixed with white”</gloss>).<!-- 1-100 items 6-8 set this stem with a final ᴇ (ɪᵋplɪˊlᴇ, Uuᵋplɪˊlᴇ, Ilh-ɪᵋplɪˊlᴇ); items 24 and 25 here set it with a final ɪ. Read as printed on both pages, checked at 4x on this one. resolved: the difference is real per editor — ᴇ on 1-100, ɪ here. Not one form miskeyed twice; both stand as printed --></item>
        <item n="25"><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" facs="#57" xml:id="term-1-101-11">ɪᵋplɪˊlɪ-keliˊlɪn</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-11">“yellowish gray spotted with white”</gloss>).</item>
        <item n="26"><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" facs="#58" xml:id="term-1-101-12">Kerg̣ɪˊpɪtku-qeˊñu</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-12">“speckled with white” [on yellowish-gray ground]</gloss>).</item>
      </list>
      <p>The name of the reindeer is generally taken from its color or from some peculiar feature of its antlers. Examples of such names are The-quite-Black-One, One-with-a-Cross-Branch-over-the-Forehead, etc. Some breeders give real names to a few reindeer of their herds: e. g., <gloss target="#name-1-101-1">Young-Lamut-Reindeer</gloss> (<name xml:id="name-1-101-1" type="zoonym" xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm">Tor-waˊwaq</name>), — though this name was given to a Chukchee reindeer partly for the purpose of increasing its agility through the force of the word, — <gloss target="#name-1-101-2">Mushroom-Eater</gloss> (<name xml:id="name-1-101-2" type="zoonym" xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm">Poˊñolɪn</name>), etc.<!-- These two replace <foreign> rather than nesting inside it: <foreign> asserts only "not the surrounding language", which xml:lang already says, and TEI treats it as the fallback where no specific element fits. The page calls them names outright ("give real names to a few reindeer"), which is what licenses <name> here, and says in the same sentence that the bearers are reindeer, which is what licenses type="zoonym". Their English renderings are left bare — English proper nouns are not this pass's work. Tor-waˊwaq breaks at the line end on the hyphen ("(Tor-" / "waˊwaq)"), so the page gives no mid-line witness for it. Retained as a connector rather than joined, per the Tier-1 hyphen rule, and the same page sets tur-rewkuteˊtɪlɪn with the cognate prefix hyphenated mid-line. resolved: keep the hyphen per editor --></p>
      <p>The term of a reindeer’s life is from twelve to fifteen years, though some animals live considerably longer. According to age, they are classified as follows: —</p>
      <list type="gloss">
        <!-- the local case, against the colour list above, which inherits its frame
             from 1-100. Drawn from the paragraph immediately above ("The term of a
             reindeer's life… According to age, they are classified as follows"):
             without it the glosses "fawn", "doe" and "buck" are generic. Follows
             the pattern the editor ruled on for the colour list rather than having
             been ruled on itself. -->
        <head><supplied reason="context" resp="#llm">of a reindeer, by age</supplied></head>
        <item><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-13">Qäyūˊ</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-13">“fawn”</gloss>), <gloss target="#term-1-101-13">both male and female</gloss>.</item>
        <item><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-14">Qliˊkin</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-14">“male fawn”</gloss>) and <term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-15">ečveˊk</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-15">“female fawn”</gloss>).</item>
        <item><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-16">Pêˊêčvak</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-16">both male and female</gloss>), <gloss target="#term-1-101-16">less than one year old</gloss>.</item>
        <item><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-17">Peˊnvel</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-17">male</gloss>) and <term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" facs="#28" xml:id="term-1-101-18">vañqaˊs˙-qor</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-18">female</gloss>), <gloss target="#term-1-101-17 #term-1-101-18">from one to two years old</gloss>.</item>
        <item><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-19">Krɪmɪˊntɪ</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-19">male</gloss>) and <term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-20">krɪˊm-qor</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-20">female</gloss>), <gloss target="#term-1-101-19 #term-1-101-20">from two to three years old</gloss>.</item>
        <item><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-21">Taačɪmɪˊntɪ</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-21">male</gloss>) and <term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" facs="#30" xml:id="term-1-101-22">ñêraˊn-ɪg̣rɪˊm-qor</term> (<gloss type="literal" target="#term-1-101-22">[literally, “two times <foreign xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm">krɪˊm-qor</foreign>”]</gloss> <gloss target="#term-1-101-22">a female</gloss>), <gloss target="#term-1-101-21 #term-1-101-22">from three to four years old</gloss>.</item>
        <item><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-23">Ñireqäˊulɪn</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-23">[“one of the second time”]</gloss> <gloss target="#term-1-101-23">male</gloss>) and <term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-24">tur-rewkuteˊtɪlɪn</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-24">[“new doe grown”]</gloss> <gloss target="#term-1-101-24">female</gloss>), <gloss target="#term-1-101-23 #term-1-101-24">from four to five years old</gloss>.</item>
        <item><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" facs="#34" xml:id="term-1-101-25">Ñĭroqaˊulɪn</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-25">[“one of the third time”]</gloss> <gloss target="#term-1-101-25">male</gloss>) and <term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-26">reˊwkut</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-26">[“doe”]</gloss> <gloss target="#term-1-101-26">female</gloss>), <gloss target="#term-1-101-25 #term-1-101-26">from five to six years old</gloss>.<!-- the opening curly quote inside the first bracket is absent on the page, confirmed at 4x; the parallel entries above and below both set it. resolved: restored per editor — the omission is the printer's, not the book's --></item>
        <item><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" facs="#36" xml:id="term-1-101-27">Ñĭraqaˊulɪn</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-27">“one of the fourth time”</gloss>), <gloss target="#term-1-101-27">male only, from six to seven years old</gloss>.</item>
        <item><term xml:lang="ckt" resp="#llm" xml:id="term-1-101-28">Čŭˊmñɪñ</term> (<gloss target="#term-1-101-28">“buck”</gloss>), <gloss target="#term-1-101-28">more than seven years old</gloss>.</item>
      </list>
      <p>The reindeer begins to shed its hair in the spring, and finishes in the middle of summer. Well-fed animals start shedding before lean ones, and grown animals before younger ones. A herd in good condition will finish two weeks earlier than a lean one. The new hair is short and sleek, but it thickens rapidly; and in September the skins of fawns are suitable for making winter garments. For this purpose skins of fawns are used almost exclusively. The fawns are killed at two set periods in early fall, according to the kind of fur desired. The late fall fawn-skins are used only for heavy overcoats; and the skins of grown animals, for sleeping-room coverings, bedding, and the like. For the Russian trade, spring fawn-skins of smaller size, called by the traders “vyporotki” (<foreign xml:lang="ru">выпоротки</foreign>), are required. Of these, the black skins are more highly valued, and bring almost three times the price of gray ones.</p>
